<GuideLayout
  title="Fabric Yardage Basics"
  description="How fabric width, panel layout, pattern repeats, and waste affect yardage for garments, curtains, upholstery, and tablecloths."
  path="/guides/fabric-yardage-basics/"
  answerFirst="Yardage is total length along the bolt after you fit pieces across the usable fabric width. If two curtain panels are each 40 inches wide and the bolt is 54 inches, you need two panel lengths, not one. Add waste for matching and mistakes—often 10%."
>
  <h2>Fabric width comes first</h2>
  <p>
    Quilting cotton is often ~42–44 inches usable; home-dec fabrics may be 54 inches; wide goods run 108 inches+.
    Always subtract selvages when deciding how many pieces fit across.
  </p>
  <h2>Panels and repeats</h2>
  <p>
    Pattern repeats force you to round each panel length up to the next multiple of the repeat.
    Plaids and large prints waste more fabric—raise the waste percentage in the
    <a href="/tools/fabric-yardage-calculator/">fabric yardage calculator</a>.
  </p>
  <h2>Project-type notes</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Curtains:</strong> multiply rod width by fullness (1.5×–3×) before calculating panels.</li>
    <li><strong>Upholstery:</strong> add for welting, directional naps, and matching.</li>
    <li><strong>Tablecloths:</strong> include drop on all sides; seam large tables thoughtfully.</li>
  </ul>
